SELECT c.site_id, 'submission', '{"email": "webdesign@contactforsales.com", "phone": "+44 (0) 7934 524 911", "domain": "webdesign.uk", "fidelity": "medium", "mission_brief": {"text": "webdesign.uk sells complete starter websites for one fixed price. It builds any sort of site, not just business sites: personal, community, club and project sites are all in scope. A customer comes to us with a domain and a description of what they want, and we build the whole site: the pages it needs, the words on them, the design, and getting it live. It is one fixed price paid once, with no ongoing fee to us and nothing the customer is locked into. Payment comes first: they pay in full before any work starts and do not see the site before paying. There is no approval stage and no revision rounds. The site is built once, and that is how the price stays down. What they get is a starter site with its initial copy written, which they are expected to edit, delivered as a ZIP of the files to keep plus the site already live at a link we host for about a month. No changes are included and we do not offer refunds. The people buying this need a decent website and have neither the time to make one nor the appetite for a drawn-out agency process. They are not technical. They have often been quoted far more than this by an agency, or have been sold a page builder subscription and never finished the site. They are sceptical, they have been oversold to before, and they respond to someone talking plainly about what they will actually get. The site should do three things. It should explain the offer clearly enough that someone understands it without asking. It should ask the visitor a little about themselves and what they are after, just a little, as an opening to a conversation rather than a form to be qualified by. And it should help them work out what to ask for, because the site is built in one pass from what they give us. The tone is plain, direct British English, written for someone who does not enjoy being sold to. Short sentences and ordinary words. This is a new service with no history to trade on, so it must not reach for the things a new service does not have. It should sound like a competent small studio that is confident about the work and relaxed about proving it, not like a startup announcing itself. Restraint reads as confidence here. Overselling would read as exactly the thing these buyers have been burnt by before. Avoid the whole register of agency marketing language: no talk of solutions, no bespoke digital experiences, no passion for pixels, no promises to transform anyone''s business. Avoid the visual cliches of the web design trade as well, the wireframe sketches and fanned colour swatches and browser windows drawn at an angle. The site is itself the portfolio piece, so what it demonstrates matters more than what it asserts."}, "roadmap_brief": {"text": "Phase one builds five pages, nothing more. The chat box and questionnaire come in a later phase, once the service behind them exists; nothing in this phase may include a chat box, an input box wired to a backend, or any form beyond a plain contact page. **index** — the home page. What webdesign.uk sells: a complete starter website, any sort of site and not just business sites, built and put live on the customer''s own domain, for one fixed price paid once before the build, with no ongoing fee and nothing the customer is locked into. Say what happens from first contact to going live in one short passage and point at how-it-works for the detail. State the price plainly, drawing it from the evidence base. No invented portfolio, no testimonials, no client counts, no years of experience — the site is new, and the page must not pretend otherwise; the site itself is the demonstration piece. **how-it-works** — the process from first email to live site, step by step, in plain words: what we need from the customer, that payment is taken in full before any work starts and they do not see the site before paying, that there is no approval stage and the site is built once, what they receive at the end (a ZIP of the files to keep plus the site already live at a link we host for about a month), and that no changes are included and we do not offer refunds. **what-you-get** — exactly what is included: the pages the site actually needs, the words on them written for it, the design, and getting it live on their own domain; and what is not included, said as plainly as what is. **faq** — the questions a sceptical buyer actually asks, answered honestly and briefly: what it costs and whether VAT applies, how long it takes, who owns the site (they do, outright), why payment comes first, why there is no approval stage and no changes are included, why we do not offer refunds, what we need from them to start, and what happens with their domain and hosting. Every number and named commitment on this page comes from the evidence base; where the evidence base holds no answer, the page says so plainly rather than inventing one. **contact** — a plain contact page: email and phone, what to put in a first message so the reply is useful, no response-time promises, no form. Later phases, recorded here for direction only and explicitly not to be built now: the short questionnaire that asks a visitor about their business and domain as an opening to a conversation, which arrives only once the chat service behind it exists; and a portfolio section, only once there is real accepted work to show."}}'::jsonb, 'owner-instruction',
  'SQL_2026-08-18e: retired commercial terms swept (owner 2026-08-18). Top-level keys unchanged.',
  true, 'webdesign_uk_build_service lane', c.pinned
FROM cur c, retire;
-- ── vertical_landscape ──
WITH cur AS (
  SELECT ss.id, ss.site_id, ss.pinned FROM site_specs ss JOIN sites s ON s.id=ss.site_id
   WHERE s.domain='webdesign.uk' AND ss.aspect='vertical_landscape' AND ss.is_current
),
retire AS (UPDATE site_specs ss SET is_current=false, superseded_at=now()
